<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Can Make a Mola. – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Beautiful Mola</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Can Make a Mola.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Explain the main ideas of class readings to absent students. concept: Use examples to explain math concepts to peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain the main ideas from a reading about molas to an absent peer by pointing to colors and shapes (e.g., points to red and says, 'Molas are colorful.'). Can use examples to help peers understand mola patterns with visuals (e.g., points to a square and says, 'This is a square in the mola.'). Can help classmates count colors in molas to practice math (e.g., points to three colors and says 'one, two, three').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling, and Clarification to prepare students for mediating information about molas and their project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures of molas, including those with animals like 'turtle', 'butterfly', 'jaguar'. Students briefly describe what they see (e.g., "It has many colors," "It is round"). The teacher guides them to recall previous lessons on mola characteristics. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher selects one mola picture and models explaining its features: "This is a mola. It has vibrant colors like red and turquoise. I like this mola because it has a beautiful butterfly design." The teacher also models describing a simple step: "First, you choose your fabric." The teacher emphasizes clear pronunciation of /m/ in 'mola' and /k/ in 'colors'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I telling you about the mola, or am I asking you a question?" (Expected: "Telling.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What am I explaining about the mola? The colors or the sounds?" (Expected: "The colors.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Beautiful Mola", focusing on how to describe the mola-making process and project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated "Virtual Mola Exhibit" project instructions or a simplified reading about the steps to create a mola. (Genre: Instructional text. Register: Informative/descriptive. Format: Short text with visuals).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing project steps using instructional language and explaining why certain design choices were made. The teacher reads a short text or displays an infographic about creating a mola, for example: "First, you choose your inspiration, maybe a turtle. Second, you cut the fabric carefully. Next, you sew the pieces to decorate. Finally, you have a unique mola!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a picture of a mola step. "Is this 'First, cut' or 'Second, sew'?" (Gist question).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"True or False: A mola has only one color." (False - checks main idea).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Match the sequence word (first, second, next) to the correct picture of a mola-making step.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation, practicing explaining information using target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using 'because' to give reasons, Present Simple for descriptions, and instructional steps (first, second, next, last). Vocabulary: 'mola', 'creativity', 'project', 'inspiration', 'cut', 'sew', 'decorate', 'vibrant', 'unique', 'carefully', 'also'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Students complete sentences about molas using 'because' and target vocabulary. E.g., "I like this mola __________ it has many shapes." "The artist chose red _________ red is a vibrant color." Teacher circulates, assisting with spelling of words like 'vibrant' and 'second'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled (Sequencing Explanations):</b> In pairs, students receive scrambled picture cards of mola-making steps. One student arranges them and explains the steps using 'First, you...', 'Next, you...', 'Last, you...'. The partner listens and helps correct the sequence or rephrases for clarity, mediating the instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Describe & Explain):</b> Students look at different mola designs (realia or pictures). In pairs, Student A describes a mola and explains *why* they like it, using "I like this design because it is colorful" or "It is unique because it has a round jaguar." Student B listens and summarizes or asks clarifying questions, then swaps roles. They also practice counting colors/shapes in the mola and explaining (e.g., "This mola has three colors: red, yellow, and blue. One, two, three.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students mediate information about their "Virtual Mola Exhibit" project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: Virtual Mola Exhibit Explanation.</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group has their "Virtual Mola Exhibit" designs (drawings or digital images of molas they've created previously). The task is to imagine an "absent student" or a "new peer" has joined their group and needs to understand the project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">One student acts as the "new peer" (or the teacher can assign this role to a different student in each group). The other students take turns explaining:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Main Ideas from Reading:</b> What is a mola? (e.g., "A mola is traditional art from Panama. It has many colors and shapes.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Their Mola Designs:</b> Describing their specific mola, pointing to colors (e.g., "This mola is golden and turquoise. I like it because it has a round turtle.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>The Project Process:</b> Explaining the steps they took to create their virtual mola design using sequencing words (e.g., "First, we chose our inspiration. Second, we drew the shapes. Next, we added vibrant colors carefully. Last, we finished our unique project.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Math Concepts:</b> Using their mola designs, they help the "new peer" count elements (e.g., "This mola has four red shapes. Let's count: one, two, three, four.").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are encouraged to use gestures, pointing, and rephrasing to ensure understanding, demonstrating collaborative art-making feedback ("Your mola is very creative!").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of students' mediation skills and reflection on their learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on effective mediation, and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will continue their "Virtual Mola Exhibit Explanation" in their groups. The teacher observes groups and provides brief feedback. Students will also perform a quick peer assessment using a simple checklist:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did my partner explain clearly? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did my partner use words like 'first', 'next', 'because'? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did my partner point to colors or shapes to help me understand? (Yes/No)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear /m/ and /k/ sounds, and accurate spelling of 'red,' 'orange,' 'vibrant,' and 'second'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (mola, creativity, project, inspiration, turtle, butterfly, jaguar, cut, sew, paste, choose, express, illustrate, decorate, collaborate, turquoise, golden, round, vibrant, traditional, unique, carefully, skillfully, artistically, also, and, because, first, second, third, next, last).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Explaining main ideas and project steps effectively.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of grammar:</b> 'because', Present simple, instructional steps.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and use of examples:</b> Helping peers understand by pointing and counting.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students write or draw answers to:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you explained well about your mola project today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What could you do better to help a friend understand next time?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Consolidating learning and planning for future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When you explained the mola project, what part was easy for you?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words or phrases did you use to help your friends understand?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What mediation strategies worked best for 4th-grade students (e.g., pointing, simple examples, sequencing words)? Most students were able to explain the steps of the project clearly using 'first, next, last,' and many successfully used 'because' to express preferences. Using their own mola designs made the explanation very personal and engaging."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for future mediation tasks? Some students still struggle with explaining abstract concepts. I need to provide more sentence frames for expressing opinions and clarifying information. Perhaps more peer modeling in the next mediation task. I also need to ensure more varied opportunities for counting and explaining mathematical patterns in other contexts."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "I Can Make a Mola" theme. The next theme will build on descriptive language and sequencing skills, applying them to another cultural project or a topic related to "My Community," where students will mediate information about local traditions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">4-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>